# Installed feed package directories to remove again, as <feed>/<dir>.
# scripts/feeds keys source packages by directory name, so two feeds carrying
# the same binary package under different directories both get installed.

# luci_smstool carries its own LuCI app, which would collide with the
# packages feed's sms-tool-js page. Only sms-tool itself is wanted.
luci_smstool/luci-app-sms-tool-js

# TTL is owned by exactly one package: luci-app-qmodem-ttlfw4 (see
# build/config). Every one of these writes its *own* nftables/iptables rules
# for TTL, so any two of them together means two rulesets rewriting the same
# packets and a LuCI page that disagrees with what is actually running.
#
#   dotycat/luci-app-nft-ttl   /etc/nftables.d/ttl64.nft, postrouting on
#                              iifname br-lan
#   qmodem/luci-app-qmodem-ttl the iptables flavour of QModem's own page; it
#                              declares PKG_CONFLICTS with the fw4 one
#   qmodem/luci-app-qmodem-ttlfw4 is the one that stays
dotycat/luci-app-nft-ttl
qmodem/luci-app-qmodem-ttl

# The nss feed's wwan/app/ tree ships its own modem managers and LuCI pages:
#   luci-app-usbmodem   depends on kmod-qmi_wwan_q, which quectel-cm selects,
#                       which makes Kconfig report a recursive dependency
#   luci-app-pcimodem   the PCIe-MHI sibling, same stack
#   luci-proto-quectel  a quectel-cm-backed protocol; QModem dials through
#                       proto qmodem_runtime now, and the quectel-cm /
#                       luci-proto-quectel packages this repo used to carry
#                       have been removed with the rest of the old modem stack
# None of them are wanted: the modem UI here is QModem. Excluding the
# directories keeps `feeds install -a` from pulling them in.
nss/luci-app-usbmodem
nss/luci-app-pcimodem
nss/luci-proto-quectel

# QModem ships two mutually exclusive main UIs: the legacy luasrc one
# (luci-app-qmodem) and the JavaScript rewrite (luci-app-qmodem-next). Only
# -next is installed - see build/config. The legacy directory is excluded so a
# dependency cannot drag it back in, which would give two menu trees writing
# the same /etc/config/qmodem.
#
# The MHI/PCIe drivers go with it: this board has a single USB modem at 4-1
# and no PCIe modem, so none of them is built. (scripts/feeds links each
# package under its own basename, so these are directory names, not paths -
# quectel_MHI_nss lives at driver/nss/ in the feed but links as
# package/feeds/qmodem/quectel_MHI_nss.)
qmodem/luci-app-qmodem
qmodem/quectel_MHI
qmodem/quectel_MHI_nss
qmodem/fibocom_QMI_WWAN
qmodem/simcom_QMI_WWAN

# Every QModem QMI driver is excluded, because this tree builds the only one
# this image may contain:
#
#   qmodem/quectel_QMI_WWAN      QModem's own qmi_wwan_q (1.5-r2). Same binary
#                                package name as wwan/qmi_wwan_q (1.5.0-r4), so
#                                building both is a duplicate-package error -
#                                and this tree's copy is the one carrying the
#                                QMAP netdev and raw-IP fixes that NSS offload
#                                depends on (docs/wwan-nss-offload.md).
#   qmodem/quectel_QMI_WWAN_nss  the same driver built against rmnet-nss, a
#                                different acceleration design from this tree's
#                                PPE glue. Nothing here uses it: qca-nss-drv
#                                binds raw-IP netdevs itself (patch 0117).
#   qmodem/rmnet-nss             rmnet_nss_callbacks. Redundant on this tree:
#                                qmi_wwan_q builds without CONFIG_QCA_NSS_DRV,
#                                so it never looks the symbol up, and the nss
#                                feed carries no rmnet-nss package of its own.
#
# QModem reaches the driver through CONFIG_PACKAGE_kmod-qmi_wwan_q=y instead;
# its DEPENDS are conditional (PACKAGE_qmodem_INCLUDE_*:kmod-...), so it never
# requires a driver of its own and these exclusions cost nothing at runtime.
qmodem/quectel_QMI_WWAN
qmodem/quectel_QMI_WWAN_nss
qmodem/rmnet-nss

# The aw1000 feed is this checkout, and $WORK lives inside it (build.sh sets
# WORK="${WORK:-$REPO/work}"). `feeds update -a` therefore scans
# aw1000-feed/work/nss-packages/ as well, which registers a *second* source
# directory for every nss package, pointing at the unmodified nss-packages
# checkout rather than at the patched copy in $WORK/nss-copy.
#
# qca-nss-drv was the one that mattered: make built
# work/nss-packages/qca-nss-drv (the aw1000 copy) while build/inside.sh
# patched feeds/nss/qca-nss-drv (the copy), so 0121 and 0122 were applied to a
# tree nothing compiled. The driver then failed on two missing prototypes in
# nss_core.c under kernel 6.18's -Werror, four hours into the build, in both
# run #16 and run #17.
#
# Excluding the aw1000 copy leaves the nss feed as the only source for these,
# which is the one that gets patched.
aw1000/qca-nss-drv
